\label{thm:I_3_5(c)}



\mainline
\bookref{Garnett}{I.3.5(c)}{Printed pages 18--19, PDF pages 30--31}
A real function $u$ on $\D$ is harmonic and nonnegative if and only if
$u=P[\mu]$ for a finite positive measure $\mu$ carried by $\partial\D$.
The measure has total mass $u(0)$.
\begin{proof}



  Use the weak-star construction in the proof of I.3.5(b), as Garnett
  does: the approximating measures $u(r\zeta)\,d\sigma$ are positive
  and have common mass $u(0)$ by the mean-value property. Their cluster
  measure is therefore positive. The converse is positivity and
  harmonicity of the Poisson integral, already formalized; existence is open.
\end{proof}

\label{thm:harmonic_mean_value}


A continuous real function on an open subset of $\mathbb C$ is harmonic
if and only if its average on every sufficiently small ball about each
point equals its value at that point. A harmonic function has this property
on every ball whose closure is contained in the domain.
\begin{proof}


  The forward mean-value property and its local converse are both stated
  in Lean; their proofs remain open.
\end{proof}

\label{thm:I_6_3}

\bookref{Garnett}{I.6.3}{Printed pages 34--35, PDF pages 46--47}
An upper semicontinuous $v:\Omega\to[-\infty,\infty)$ is subharmonic if
and only if, for every bounded open $W$ compactly contained in $\Omega$
and every harmonic $u$ on $W$, the boundary condition
$\limsup_{W\ni z\to\zeta}(v(z)-u(z))\le0$ implies $v\le u$ on $W$.
\begin{proof}



  Apply the maximum principle to $v-u$. Conversely, approximate the
  upper semicontinuous boundary data from above by continuous functions,
  solve the Dirichlet problems, and pass to the circular mean inequality;
  integrating in the radius gives the ball mean inequality.
  The active Lean definition uses comparison on balls; the equivalence
  with the book's mean-value definition remains to be formalized.
\end{proof}

\label{thm:log_abs_subharmonic}


If $f$ is analytic on an open subset of $\mathbb{C}$, then
$z\mapsto \log \lVert f(z)\rVert$, interpreted as $-\infty$ at zeros, is
subharmonic there. This has been formalized both for complex-valued
functions and, more generally, for functions taking values in a complex
Banach space. Since the maximum of two subharmonic functions is
subharmonic, so is $\log^+\lVert f\rVert=\max(\log\lVert f\rVert,0)$.
\begin{proof}



  The Lean proofs are complete in the declarations linked above.
\end{proof}

\label{cor:I_6_4}

\bookref{Garnett}{I.6.4}{Printed page 35, PDF page 47}
If $v$ is subharmonic on a connected open set $\Omega$ and
$v\not\equiv-\infty$, then its restriction to every circle bounding a
closed disc in $\Omega$ is integrable and has finite mean.
\begin{proof}



  Continuous boundary majorants and the positive Poisson kernel show that
  a circle mean of $-\infty$ would force $v=-\infty$ throughout its disc.
  The set where $v$ is locally $-\infty$ is then open and closed, contrary
  to connectedness and the hypothesis. Upper semicontinuity bounds the
  positive part on the circle.
\end{proof}

